1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,616
A schoolteacher once set a class a long, dull task: add every number from one to a hundred.

2
00:00:06,616 --> 00:00:09,999
One boy, Carl Gauss, had the answer in seconds.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,845
Here is his trick, with one to ten.

4
00:00:12,845 --> 00:00:16,465
Write the numbers forwards, then backwards underneath.

5
00:00:16,465 --> 00:00:18,952
Every column adds to eleven.

6
00:00:18,952 --> 00:00:23,939
Ten columns of eleven is a hundred and ten, but that is the sum twice.

7
00:00:23,939 --> 00:00:25,788
So the sum is fifty five.

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,015
For one to a hundred: a hundred columns of a hundred and one, halved.

9
00:00:31,015 --> 00:00:32,746
Five thousand and fifty.

10
00:00:32,746 --> 00:00:43,344
In this unit you will find formulas like this for sums of squares and cubes, prove them for every n at once, and meet two series that never end but still add up.

11
00:00:48,833 --> 00:00:53,903
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 4: Sequence and Series.

12
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:57,395
Four parts.

13
00:00:57,395 --> 00:01:01,549
The sum of the first n natural numbers and sigma notation.

14
00:01:01,549 --> 00:01:07,408
Sums of squares and cubes, and a method for any series with a polynomial term.

15
00:01:07,408 --> 00:01:09,661
Proof by mathematical induction.

16
00:01:09,661 --> 00:01:12,347
And the exponential and logarithmic series.

17
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:19,605
The capital Greek letter sigma means add up.

18
00:01:19,605 --> 00:01:26,524
Below it, where the counter k starts; on top, where it stops; on the right, the term to add each time.

19
00:01:26,733 --> 00:01:35,393
Sigma k squared, k from one to five: put k equal to one, two, three, four, five in turn and add.

20
00:01:35,393 --> 00:01:36,669
Fifty five.

21
00:01:36,669 --> 00:01:42,320
The letter k is only a counter, a dummy: use r instead and nothing changes.

22
00:01:43,533 --> 00:01:47,128
Two rules, used in every question in this unit.

23
00:01:47,128 --> 00:01:49,734
Sigma splits over a sum or difference.

24
00:01:49,967 --> 00:01:51,935
A constant comes out in front.

25
00:01:51,935 --> 00:01:57,026
And a constant on its own, added n times, gives n times the constant.

26
00:01:57,233 --> 00:01:59,292
But there is no rule for a product.

27
00:01:59,292 --> 00:02:02,834
The sum of a k b k is not the product of the two sums.

28
00:02:04,067 --> 00:02:06,972
Here is the same trick as a picture.

29
00:02:06,972 --> 00:02:12,470
Stack one dot, two dots, three, up to six: a triangle of twenty one dots.

30
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:16,217
Make a copy, turn it upside down, and slide it in.

31
00:02:16,217 --> 00:02:22,102
The two triangles lock together into a rectangle, n rows by n plus one columns.

32
00:02:22,333 --> 00:02:32,035
So twice the sum is n times n plus one, and the sum of the first n natural numbers is n into n plus one, over two.

33
00:02:32,035 --> 00:02:37,521
These are the triangular numbers: one, three, six, ten, and so on.

34
00:02:38,733 --> 00:02:39,532
Two quick ones.

35
00:02:39,967 --> 00:02:51,768
For a sum that starts part way along, take the full sum to a hundred and subtract the sum to fifty: five thousand and fifty minus twelve seventy five, three thousand seven hundred and seventy five.

36
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,530
The k th odd number is two k minus one.

37
00:02:55,530 --> 00:03:00,022
Split with the sigma rules: twice the sum of k, minus n.

38
00:03:00,022 --> 00:03:01,611
That is n squared.

39
00:03:01,611 --> 00:03:06,780
One plus three plus five plus seven is sixteen, four squared.

40
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:07,221
Check.

41
00:03:09,233 --> 00:03:10,581
Work backwards from a total.

42
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:15,648
Set n into n plus one over two equal to four sixty five.

43
00:03:15,867 --> 00:03:20,921
Solve the quadratic: root of three seven two one is sixty one.

44
00:03:20,921 --> 00:03:23,452
So n is thirty or minus thirty one.

45
00:03:23,667 --> 00:03:26,937
A count of terms cannot be negative: thirty.

46
00:03:26,937 --> 00:03:32,913
And if no positive whole-number root appears, the answer is that no such n exists.

47
00:03:32,913 --> 00:03:33,605
Never round.

48
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:43,525
For the sum of squares we use a beautiful trick called telescoping.

49
00:03:43,525 --> 00:03:52,174
Start from an identity: k plus one, cubed, minus k cubed, equals three k squared plus three k plus one.

50
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:56,948
Write it out for k equal to one, two, three, all the way to n.

51
00:03:57,167 --> 00:03:59,005
Now add all the lines.

52
00:03:59,005 --> 00:04:06,908
On the left, plus two cubed cancels minus two cubed, three cubed cancels three cubed, and so on down the column.

53
00:04:06,908 --> 00:04:15,659
Like a telescope folding shut, everything collapses except the last and the first: n plus one, cubed, minus one.

54
00:04:15,867 --> 00:04:22,470
On the right: three times the sum of squares, plus three times the sum of k, plus n.

55
00:04:22,470 --> 00:04:32,663
We already know the sum of k. Rearrange, and the sum of the first n squares is n, n plus one, two n plus one, over six.

56
00:04:33,867 --> 00:04:35,457
The cubes have a picture too.

57
00:04:35,457 --> 00:04:41,551
Take squares whose sides are the triangular numbers: one, three, six, ten.

58
00:04:41,551 --> 00:04:44,732
Each new square adds an L shaped strip.

59
00:04:44,933 --> 00:04:50,260
The strip added at stage two has area nine minus one, eight: two cubed.

60
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:56,198
At stage three, thirty six minus nine, twenty seven: three cubed.

61
00:04:56,198 --> 00:05:01,758
At stage four, a hundred minus thirty six, sixty four: four cubed.

62
00:05:01,758 --> 00:05:07,551
So one plus eight plus twenty seven plus sixty four fills a ten by ten square.

63
00:05:07,767 --> 00:05:17,013
In general the sum of the first n cubes is the square of n into n plus one over two: the square of the sum of the first n numbers.

64
00:05:17,013 --> 00:05:21,667
The same telescoping trick, with fourth powers, proves it algebraically.

65
00:05:22,900 --> 00:05:24,790
The three standard sums.

66
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:28,553
Memorise the denominators: two, six, four.

67
00:05:28,553 --> 00:05:30,738
They are the commonest slip under pressure.

68
00:05:30,967 --> 00:05:44,591
And the method for any series whose term is a polynomial: write the k th term, split it with sigma rules, substitute the three sums, factorise, and always test your answer on n equals one or two.

69
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:47,211
Direct substitution.

70
00:05:47,433 --> 00:05:53,210
Twenty, twenty one, forty one, over six: two thousand eight hundred and seventy.

71
00:05:53,433 --> 00:06:02,208
For the cubes, square the triangular number: fifteen times sixteen over two is a hundred and twenty; squared, fourteen thousand four hundred.

72
00:06:04,233 --> 00:06:05,209
The classic shape.

73
00:06:05,467 --> 00:06:10,618
The k th term is k times k plus one: k squared plus k.

74
00:06:10,833 --> 00:06:15,863
Sum each piece and take out the common factor n into n plus one over six.

75
00:06:16,067 --> 00:06:23,959
The bracket is two n plus four, so the sum is n, n plus one, n plus two, over three.

76
00:06:23,959 --> 00:06:30,620
Test with n equal to two: eight, and one times two plus two times three is eight.

77
00:06:30,833 --> 00:06:33,371
For twenty terms: three thousand and eighty.

78
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:36,711
Squares of odd numbers.

79
00:06:36,933 --> 00:06:40,867
The k th odd number is two k minus one; square it.

80
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:47,355
Split, substitute and simplify: n times four n squared minus one, over three.

81
00:06:47,567 --> 00:06:50,264
Factorise the difference of squares.

82
00:06:50,264 --> 00:06:53,627
For n equal to ten: thirteen hundred and thirty.

83
00:06:55,633 --> 00:06:57,268
A sum that starts part way along.

84
00:06:57,500 --> 00:07:00,457
Subtract the sum up to ten from the sum up to twenty.

85
00:07:00,457 --> 00:07:04,420
Up to ten, not eleven: eleven cubed must stay in.

86
00:07:04,633 --> 00:07:10,611
Two hundred and ten squared minus fifty five squared: forty one thousand and seventy five.

87
00:07:13,233 --> 00:07:14,615
Exam tip.

88
00:07:14,615 --> 00:07:20,567
To sum from m to n, subtract the sum up to m minus one from the sum up to n. Subtract up to m itself and you throw away a term you were meant to keep.

